    "## 2. Model / Algorithm Justification\n",
    "\n",
    "The client needs two things, so we use two models:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Task | Model | Why |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Identify areas of concern | **LDA (Latent Dirichlet Allocation)** | Unsupervised — no labels needed. Groups complaint text into topics by their most common words (Blei, Ng and Jordan, 2003).|\n",
    "| Classify sentiment | **Logistic Regression** vs **Multinomial Naive Bayes** | Both are fast, interpretable baselines for sparse TF-IDF text. We train both and choose the better one. |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Alternatives considered:**\n",
    "\n",
    "| Model | Pros | Cons |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Naive Bayes | Fast, simple | Strong word-independence assumption |\n",
    "| Logistic Regression | Interpretable, strong baseline | Assumes linear boundary |\n",
    "| Random Forest | Handles non-linear patterns | Slow on high-dimensional text, less interpretable |\n",
    "| SVM | Strong NLP performance | Computationally expensive to tune |\n",
    "\n",
    "We chose Logistic Regression and Naive Bayes because they are the two strongest and most efficient baselines for TF-IDF data, and comparing them lets us make an evidence-based final choice."
   ]
